And it's like, that's really cool.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:07.500
It's jazzy. It's groovy.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:09.520
And it feels victorious.

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:12.580
But I also just like the Ice Climber theme in general.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:16.340
Like, I thought that that was, like, a cool, like, it was, it had its own feel to it.

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.920
Yeah, it was jazzy, like you said.

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:20.560
It's what, that's kind of what I got.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:21.760
It was, like, a very, like, jazzy soundtrack.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.760
It felt like all the parts of the game was there except for the gameplay.

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:26.640
You know, the music was there.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:27.700
The visuals were there.

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:29.100
Yeah. It just wasn't that fun.

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:34.560
I do want to go back real quick to, like, just, like, the, again, the objective of the game.

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:37.420
We basically said what the objective is.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:39.860
Like, hey, we want to go up and get your fruit.

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:41.400
Or not your fruit. Your vegetables.

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:46.200
I'm sorry. But in what world does that make any sense?

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:50.420
I mean, like, it seems to be, like, based in reality in some regard.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:56.140
But this bird, this condor that steals your vegetables.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:00.160
Like what? It sounds like it's based off of, like, some ancient myth.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:01.460
Yeah? Something like that. You know?

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:03.520
I feel like that's way too much credit.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:09.140
That or just, like, a bunch of really drugged out developers.

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:12.320
Yeah. Definitely wasn't a plot heavy game.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:20.080
Right. Yeah. And one thing I was going to mention is the two player mode that Joe was talking about there for a second.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:25.760
I thought that was kind of cool because it makes it kind of co-op in a way until you get to the top.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:30.280
And then all of a sudden it becomes, like, oh, we have to, like, you know, now you have to fight to get to the vegetables.

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:35.200
Right. And we played it kind of co-op at first because we were just both so bad at it initially.

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:40.580
And then after a while we kind of realized, like, we should probably be competing because it shows our scores at the end.

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:48.400
I think it got a little more interesting when we were competing because if you go up too high above your competitor, they disappear off the screen and lose a life.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.620
So it's kind of like a race at that point, too.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:56.180
But the annoying thing about Call of Duty, I played with my roommate who was not very good.

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.780
I mean, I wasn't good, but she was also not very good.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:01.880
If you lose all your lives, they're just out.

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:02.860
For the rest of the game.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:05.140
Yeah. I think that's the case.

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:06.600
Yeah. You just guys in there.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:09.760
But it's kind of like when you're playing any other two player games.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.140
And it's like, once you lose your lives, it's like, what do you do?

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:13.060
You just watch the guy.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:16.280
But, like, after the next level, like, you're still out.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.320
No, no, I am. I'm saying, like, think about it like this, too.

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:21.900
If you're playing with, like, your older brother who's really good at that game, right?

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:26.840
Not Ice Climbers specifically, but let's do, like, a Kung Fu or something like that.

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:32.080
If they're really good at the game, you have to watch them play a majority of the game, and then they might die.

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:33.820
And then it's your turn to go.

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:34.920
You have no idea what you're doing.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:38.560
You die, and then it's back to watching them for another, like, 10, 15 minutes.

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:48.400
Yeah. Well, yeah, I thought that the way that that works with Ice Climbers would only really work if you're both equally as good at the game.

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:52.260
So then, like, if I die, I don't have to watch the other person too much longer.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:55.460
They're just trying to rack up as many points to try and beat my score.

00:14:55.720 --> 00:15:01.520
But if it's, like, one person significantly better than the other, and, like, I lose all three lives, and then they go on for the next 10 mountains,

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:06.860
kind of defeats the purpose of multiplayer because there's not much of a chance that I got a higher score than them.

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:14.260
Yeah, and Sean and I, Sean got a little aggressive with me when we were playing and figured out very quickly that you want to get to the top faster than the other player,

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:16.160
and you don't necessarily help each other.

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:23.500
Yeah, because I think I just disagree with you at a basic level of, like, your idea of it being co-op in the beginning.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:24.960
Like, this is not a co-op game.

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:33.080
Like, yeah, it'll be faster because there are two players hitting the bricks to get up, so there's just more surface area available.

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:35.820
And that ends up sort of helping both of us.

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:38.080
But, yeah, I'm not trying to make a hole for you.

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:39.640
I'm trying to push you off those ledges.

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.880
Any stray thoughts about Ice Climber before we move on?

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:56.680
One thing I noticed was that you—so I think I got maybe to, like, Mountain 11 or 12 or something.

00:15:57.260 --> 00:15:58.180
Maybe it was more than that.

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:02.180
I don't remember. But then once I died, I realized that you can just choose any level.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.220
You don't have to earn it.

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:07.460
You can just skip to the, whatever, the 30th mountain or—

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:08.880
Yeah, 30th mountain.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:11.100
You just select whichever mountain you want from the beginning.

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:15.400
So I thought that was interesting that there wasn't, like, a progression in that sense.

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:19.380
That was something Sean just showed me before this podcast, actually.

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:22.400
He was like, yeah, well, it doesn't really matter because we can just go to any level.

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:24.260
I'm like, what do you mean we can go to any level?

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:26.980
Like, it felt like—I don't know.

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:30.560
It feels like a nice reward, though, if you don't want to, like, have to play through it all.

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:33.480
You know? Yeah. If you think about 30 mountains, it's a lot.

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:37.040
But also, like, you kind of don't want to play through it all.

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.300
You know? Yeah, but I did enjoy the taste.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.620
I enjoyed the taste of seeing what Mountain 30 was.

00:16:41.620 --> 00:16:44.660
Right, yeah, yeah. And we were like, oh, thank goodness we didn't try to get this far.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:45.740
We couldn't get off the ground floor.

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:49.040
And I just think it's kind of weird on an NES game.

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:53.240
Like, you don't see that very often because NES games are usually so much more punishing than, like, games now.

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:58.520
It was weird that you didn't have to, like—you didn't have to get really, really good at this game to ever get to Mountain 30.

00:16:58.740 --> 00:16:59.560
You could just start there.

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:03.720
Right, okay. But it's kind of weird that they're not, like, unlocked or anything like that.

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:05.420
I don't know. That's kind of odd.

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:14.480
Well, this might be a good segue to talk about the verse system version of Ice Climber, which was the—you know, like, the arcade versions always had the verse system.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.120
Which kind of came out first in America, but they came after the Famicom versions.

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:28.080
The verse Ice Climber, the difference there is that you kind of, like, you choose a selection of mountains out of—I think it's, like, 48 this time instead of, whatever, 30 or something like that.

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:29.680
It might be 32 instead of 30.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:35.620
But you choose, like, a selection of eight, you clear those, and then you have to choose—like, you choose another set of eight.

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:37.660
But you get to kind of, like—you get to kind of, like, choose.

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:40.620
And I didn't get to see it because it's very hard to see these systems.

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:47.000
But, like, that'd be kind of cool if it was, like, an overworld or something and you, like, chose a series of mountains or something like that.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:48.920
I'm not sure how it was done. It was probably just done through text.

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:53.200
But I thought there was, like, a neat little addition to, like, choose your set of mountains.

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:58.660
Yeah, and then, like, you could try to pick the mountains that you think you'll do better on than your opponent.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:02.800
I guess, like, you could pick each opposite one.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:03.860
Right, right. Yeah.

00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:07.260
A little bit of strategy to beat the other guy.

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:26.920
Yeah. So, guys, I kind of want to know, we had a lot of fun with that Duck Hunt and what would a modern Duck Hunt—what would Duck Hunt 2 on Nintendo Switch look like?

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:30.360
And I just think that would be a fun thing to kind of keep bringing back, maybe.

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:34.340
So, Ice Climber hasn't gotten a sequel ever.

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:35.560
Probably won't.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:39.220
But if it did, what does that look like?

00:18:39.220 --> 00:18:52.760
I mean, the easy way, like, the lame, boring one would just be, like, open-world Ice Climber, and you're just trying to climb all these mountains that you're just in, like, you're just in a mountain range.

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:57.620
I know, but I see where you're going there, and I was actually thinking, like—so, I had two thoughts.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:00.620
And, Sam, I think one of them is going to impede with yours.

00:19:00.620 --> 00:19:04.060
But my first one was thinking Super Smash Bros.

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:10.260
Melee had that level of Ice Climber where it was kind of like an event, you know, and it would just keep scrolling and you go through.

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:12.900
And that's like, okay, that's kind of a cool way to handle it.

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:19.180
But then when you started talking about, like, a progression or something, I was like, what if Ice Climber became, like, a Metroidvania?

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:27.920
You know, where it was like you're going through this mountain and you have to, like, get certain new power-ups to, you know, to get to new parts of the mountain.

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:32.400
And, you know, like, you can kind of take on the mountain in different routes.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:33.720
What if it was also—

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:35.120
But the objective is to get to the top.

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:38.340
Do you ever play, like, SteamWorld Dig or anything like that?

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:42.400
You play that one? Where you go, like, underground and you keep going down and down and down and down.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:43.960
As far as you can, and then you go back up.

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:46.360
Yeah, and that might be a cool way of exploring it.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:52.460
And what if it was a Metroidvania that was designed to be played in a co-op?

00:19:52.460 --> 00:20:05.080
So it is actually a co-op game this time, and there are certain ways or, like, puzzles in, like, the openings and you're not just digging that you can only get through if—

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:08.280
Like, in a—sort of like a—I know that Brothers, like the new—

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:12.000
Yeah, yeah, yeah. —is designed to be played one player but with, like, two.

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:13.640
But you can play that with two players.

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:20.480
And you had to get through these puzzles by having these two characters cooperate in some way.

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:23.840
I think that would be an interesting concept to explore.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:29.440
Yeah. I actually thought that this was going to be co-op because of Super Smash Bros.

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:32.580
So it's interesting—because in Super Smash Bros., they work together, right?

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:34.380
They're like a team.

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:35.460
Yeah, they're a team.

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:37.980
Right. And they're kind of tied together.

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:47.980
Yeah. Right. So it would be interesting if it was that kind of mechanic, like two players that are tied together in some way having to climb these mountains, I guess.

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:49.940
Honestly, I'd kind of play this game.

00:20:50.420 --> 00:20:53.780
Yeah. A co-op metroidvania.

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:56.240
Get to the top of the mountain.

00:20:56.420 --> 00:21:00.980
Like, there's no real, like—I mean, there might be a boss or something like that down the line, but there's no real story.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:02.620
It's just kind of like, you're the ice climber.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:07.560
Get to the top of the mountain as a team and use your, you know, newfound power-ups.

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:10.600
And it's like, it doesn't matter how you get to—like, I could say it as a cool speedrunning game.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:12.580
Like, it doesn't matter how you get to the top of the mountain.

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:16.220
It could even be like a roguelike or something where the mountain, like, generates—

00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:17.080
Like a Spelunky?

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:23.280
Yeah, yeah. Like, it'll have different paths depending on—what's the word I'm looking for?

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:26.160
Procedurally generated. Procedurally generated, yeah.

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:28.220
Procedurally generated mountain, yeah.

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:29.880
So is this 3D?

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:32.780
I see it as 2D. Well, I think it's like 2.5.

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:36.180
2.5D. So you're not actually moving in, like, Z space.

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:43.220
Yeah, I think this could even work on, like, a 3DS level, kind of like how the Metroid Samus Returns went,

00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:47.820
where it was like, okay, this is, like, not too important, but we have the ability to make this,

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:49.320
so let's put it out on 3DS.

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:52.440
Like, I'd be fine with that, but I think this is a pretty cool game.

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:54.620
Yeah. Ice Clammer 2, Breath of the Wild.

00:21:56.360 --> 00:21:57.880
Is that what you're going to subtitle them all?

00:21:58.540 --> 00:21:59.940
No. Okay. I hope not.

00:22:00.620 --> 00:22:03.260
Oh, yeah, didn't we call Duck Hunt Breath of the Wild, though?

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:04.600
Duck Hunt 2, Breath of the Wild.

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:06.760
Yeah, I think that was thrown around at some point.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:11.580
All right, so I think it's time to move into the Essential Games list.

00:22:14.960 --> 00:22:16.880
All right, so it's the Essential Games list.

00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:17.900
I'm sure you know by now.

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:20.520
We're chilling in on, like, what is this, episode...

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:22.400
Fridge. I don't really know.

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:24.280
I don't know. I said Fridge, for the record.

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:25.500
I just wanted everyone to know that.

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:26.960
You're on episode 9.

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:30.580
You should know by now, but basically, you got your PS4, you got your Nintendo Switch,

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:35.220
you got your PS2 with Shadow of the Colossus on, and you're like, oh, I want to get that

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:40.200
PS4 version of Shadow of the Colossus, and it's like, wait a minute, Ice Climber on NES

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:42.180
exists. Should I play that?

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:44.840
Well, that's what the Essential Games list is here to tell you.

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:49.540
Is it worth playing right now when you can play Shadow of the Colossus on PS4 instead?

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:53.800
You know, I didn't see the similarity until right now, but you climb.

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:56.120
You climb. Yeah, you do climb, yeah.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:59.240
And maybe that was part of the Ice Climber's modern version, too.

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:00.900
You just climb a Colossus instead of mountains.

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:02.580
Right. It's just Shadow of the Colossus.

00:23:03.100 --> 00:23:05.000
Anyway, let's kind of go around.

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:09.340
For those of, again, if you're not acquainted, we do a vote here, and basically you need at

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:12.120
least two to get into the Essential Games list, because there's four of us.

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:14.400
Sean, take us through Essential Games list.

00:23:14.540 --> 00:23:16.340
You should just play Shadow of the Colossus.

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:27.840
Um, I know that this game has a lot of importance in just the history of the NES, but don't go

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:34.060
back and play this unless you want a lesson in frustration between janky jump mechanics

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:37.760
and hit detection and all that stuff.

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:39.800
All right. Well said and shortly spoken.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:46.000
Joe? Yeah, so I definitely agree that the jump mechanics and the hit detection were both, like,

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:47.560
big flaws in this game.

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:52.140
I don't think I had as bad an experience as everyone else seemed to.

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:53.280
I thought it was fun.

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:55.580
I had a good time playing it, but definitely not essential.

00:23:56.020 --> 00:23:57.540
Definitely not worth going out of the way to play.

00:23:57.800 --> 00:23:58.660
Uh, yeah. Nah.

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:01.060
I, this one's not it.

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:05.500
Nah. It's just, it looks good, it sounds good, but it's just not fun.

00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:07.980
I don't know. I just, it's not going on the list.

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:10.920
But the jump, it's just, is the main point of the game.

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:13.900
And it's, it's really hard and frustrating to jump.

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:16.940
And it's just, I mean, we were talking about this before.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:20.980
I mean, Super Mario Brothers eclipses this so, so much.

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:25.020
But Mike was telling me, uh, well, yeah, you can say it.

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:30.620
The main programmer, yeah. The main programmer for Super Mario Brothers is the programmer here on, uh, Ice Climber.

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:32.020
Yeah. And so that's just weird.

00:24:32.260 --> 00:24:36.320
And I also noticed the jump kind, like, it sounds a little bit like the Mario jump too.

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:37.580
Right. It has that little, like, boah.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:39.540
Right. Which I guess is like, yeah.

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:43.520
I've always thought that Super Mario Brothers obviously has, like, the best jump mechanics

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:46.620
on, you know, Super Mario Brothers and Super Mario Brothers 3 have great jump mechanics.

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:50.280
But the actual, just original Mario Brothers, which kind of looks a little bit like this

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:53.540
game, jump mechanics I thought were kind of janky in that too.

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:54.900
It's actually what this reminded me of.

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:56.160
Are you skipping ahead, Joe?

00:24:56.500 --> 00:24:57.680
No, but I, I, I.

00:24:57.680 --> 00:24:59.360
Well, no, we're talking about Super Mario Brothers.

00:24:59.500 --> 00:25:00.880
That's ahead as well. Right.

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:02.980
I think, I think I understand what Joe's saying though.

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:06.980
And I actually, you bring up a great point that a similarity I didn't really notice is

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:12.120
like the idea of, you know, hitting the blocks in Ice Climber and hitting the Koopas from

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:14.900
below you by hitting the platforms in Mario Brothers.

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:17.260
They are kind of similar. And you are right.

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:20.660
The Mario Brothers controls very much like the Donkey Kong controls too.

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:23.560
It's a very tight system when it comes to the jump.

00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:29.080
I'm just so surprised that these games launched together, Ice Climber and Super Mario Brothers.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:34.680
But you, you know, you don't really like, they're two totally different games because, you know,

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:36.740
forget the fact that Ice Climber is more arcadey.

00:25:36.920 --> 00:25:38.180
It's, it's the jump.

00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:39.780
The jump changes this game.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:42.440
And that's why I'm also a no on the essential games list here.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:45.520
Because the concept is pretty fun and simple.

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:46.600
Get to the top of the mountain.

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:50.820
I like the music, which is like, you know, it's way ahead of its time, I think, in terms

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:54.900
of like having an actual song to identify the Ice Climber as.

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:59.860
But the gameplay, I, I thought I was going to be able to get over it.

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:05.380
Couldn't. And it never made me want to go back because every time I was like, oh, I'm sure this game

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:06.760
is good. Let me play it again.

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:07.920
And I go play it again.

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:09.040
And I immediately remember.

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:10.240
Yeah. You jump once.

00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:11.860
And all of a sudden it's like, oh my gosh.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:15.500
Like someone release a ROM hack that fixes the jump.

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:20.000
So like going back to one of our earlier episodes, like when I was playing Clu Clu Land, like

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.660
I, I had some nice things to say about that game, but while I was playing it, I'm just

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:29.460
like, man, I can't, I can't wait until we get further into like the library of the system.

00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:34.720
So there's just more to do because I mean, it was, it's a very basic game.

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:38.420
There, there is some depth to it, but you're really just going left, right, up, down.

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:44.820
And then fight, like, this is one of the first just like character based action games that

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:49.680
we've played and there's a lot to do, but the execution is just so frustrating.

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:52.500
I was, honestly, I was really excited for this one.

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:54.500
It just didn't, that's it.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:56.520
I was really excited. It just was not as fun.

00:26:56.540 --> 00:26:59.040
Can I ask you guys a question just out of curiosity?

00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:04.640
If, if the, the jump mechanics and the kind of clipping through walls were fixed, would

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:05.760
you guys find this fun?

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:07.140
This would be a totally different game.

00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:08.280
Yeah. I would love this game.

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:11.440
Yeah. Maybe, I mean, you're still just jumping though.

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:13.080
Right. Yeah. There's still not much to it.

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:16.120
Maybe if the jump was better, it would also be easier to get through the mountains and

00:27:16.120 --> 00:27:17.020
it would feel better.

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:19.360
And I would want to continue to climb up those mountains.

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:23.100
Right. When you get to mountain seven and you get game over and you have to start back at the

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:26.200
beginning, unless you do the mountain cheat, but even just like starting back at the beginning,

00:27:26.320 --> 00:27:29.620
it's like, I don't really want to climb these mountains again knowing what I'm up against

00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:30.720
with my own controller.

00:27:31.100 --> 00:27:35.200
I think the important thing for me was, was playing two player because playing one player,

00:27:35.380 --> 00:27:38.680
it was very frustrating because I'm like, ah, like what's, what's, what's the point of

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:42.000
going through this and like having all this trouble and like, oh, it's not working in this

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:45.420
way. But when I'm playing against someone else, we're both trying to overcome, it's almost

00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:48.740
like the bad controls are part of what makes it work.

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:51.200
Yeah. Would that be a jank folder?

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:53.020
It might be the jank folder.

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:58.300
Like take a sip, take a sip every time you, uh, your partner, you know, like doesn't make

00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:00.580
it up to your level or something like that and they get work to you.

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:02.760
It might go in the jank folder, but I don't know.

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:05.140
It's, it's not full jank, you know?

00:28:05.820 --> 00:28:09.460
All right. So, so it's not essential and that's where we land off.

